<speak><prosody pitch="0.14st" rate="1.00">The classic paper airplane!</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="-1.28st" rate="1.01">Making one is a simple and fun activity that can provide hours of entertainment.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.46st" rate="0.98">Here&apos;s a step-by-step guide on how to make a basic paper airplane: **Materials:** * One piece of paper (preferably 8.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.41st" rate="0.98">5 x 11 inches) **Instructions:** 1.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.94st" rate="1.04">**Start with a flat sheet:** Hold the paper flat with one end facing you.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="0.64st" rate="0.99">2.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="1.35st" rate="1.02">**Fold the top corner down:** Fold the top right and left corners down so that they meet at the middle crease.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="0.10st" rate="1.01">The fold should be about 1 inch deep.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="0.70st" rate="1.02">3.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="1.30st" rate="1.00">**Create the wing:** Next, fold the top edge of the paper down to create the wing.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="1.40st" rate="0.98">The fold should be about 0.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.74st" rate="1.02">5 inches deep and should be parallel to the bottom edge of the paper.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.50st" rate="1.02">4.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="0.13st" rate="0.99">**Repeat on the other side:** Fold the top edge of the paper down again to create the second wing.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.42st" rate="1.02">Make sure it&apos;s symmetrical with the first wing.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="0.06st" rate="1.00">5.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="0.03st" rate="1.00">**Form the nose:** Fold the front edge of the paper up to create the nose of the airplane.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.76st" rate="1.01">The fold should be about 0.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="0.58st" rate="1.00">25 inches deep.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.70st" rate="1.00">6.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="0.50st" rate="1.01">**Create the tail:** Fold the back edge of the paper up to create the tail.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.48st" rate="1.02">The fold should be about 0.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="1.22st" rate="0.99">25 inches deep and should be parallel to the front edge.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.92st" rate="0.99">7.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="1.19st" rate="1.01">**Give it some shape:** Gently push the wings down towards the body of the airplane to give it a slight curve.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.90st" rate="1.00">This will help with stability during flight.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.59st" rate="1.03">8.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="0.87st" rate="1.01">**Your plane is ready!</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="0.34st" rate="1.01">** Give your airplane a gentle toss forward to test its aerodynamics.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="0.56st" rate="0.98">If necessary, make any adjustments to the wings or tail to improve its performance.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="0.63st" rate="1.04">Tips: * Use a heavier weight paper for better flight performance.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.17st" rate="1.00">* Experiment with different folds and designs to create unique variations of the classic paper airplane.</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="-0.32st" rate="1.04">* Practice makes perfect!</prosody><break time="520ms"/><prosody pitch="-1.15st" rate="1.00">Don&apos;t be discouraged if your first few attempts at making a paper airplane don&apos;t fly so well – just keep trying!</prosody><break time="520ms"/><break time="120ms"/><prosody pitch="1.12st" rate="0.98">Now, go ahead and give flying a try!</prosody><break time="520ms"/></speak>